WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.689
It's great to welcome to the program today,
David Roberts, who's a [inaudible]

00:00:03.689 --> 00:00:09.900
journalists for Vox, focusing on climate change
and politics. And we're going to talk about,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.340
uh, we're going to talk about a lot of different
things. David, great to have you on today.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:14.860
Glad to be here.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:20.040
So a lot of the folks in my audience have
been in a concerned manner following what

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:26.270
seems to be the sort of the irrelevance of
the truth as it may be in modern politics,

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:30.760
whether it's alternative facts, whether it's
there's nothing Donald Trump could ever do,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.429
even hypothetically, that would make me reconsider
my support for him, which we heard is the

00:00:35.429 --> 00:00:44.109
case for 62% of Trump supporters. Some are
just calling this a post truth status quo

00:00:44.109 --> 00:00:49.859
in which we find ourselves, but is, is that
encompassing enough? How do you describe what

00:00:49.859 --> 00:00:54.249
you see today? Is the relationship between
the sort of political world and the truth

00:00:54.249 --> 00:00:55.399
as it is knowable?

00:00:55.399 --> 00:01:02.870
Sure. Well, that's a, that's a, that's a lot,
but it's complicated. But I guess the way

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:09.880
I would put it is that, um, in any sort of
society or culture, there's a, uh, bedrock

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:14.700
of trust, what, what, what they call social
trust. Political scientists call it social

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.439
trust, which is just a sense that we're, we're
in this together, that we're part of, we're

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:26.570
there, we're part of a larger project together
and that whatever sacrifices we make for that

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:31.650
project, whatever restraints we put on ourselves
or whatever, um, you know, we have to do,

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:37.710
we get back from that project at, you know,
we get food, shelter, uh, a social belonging.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:44.890
So social trust is sort of the basis, uh,
it turns out for everything for politics working.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:52.530
Um, and it turns out even to be the basis
of, uh, our information system. So what we've

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:58.730
seen happen in the last several decades is,
uh, a sort of erosion of social trust in the

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:07.439
U S um, I have my own thoughts about who was,
who and what were the primary cause of that.

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:12.540
But the result is that the institution of
media, the institution of journalism, uh,

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:19.480
as it existed in postwar America, no longer
commands, widespread social trust. There,

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:25.780
there is a large faction of the country, 30,
40%, no one I think knows exactly how large

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:31.300
that simply does not accept what, uh, what
mainstream journalists journalism tells it

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:38.610
anymore and has now, um, you know, the, the,
the, the conservative movement lost trust

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:44.610
in mainstream media, separated from it more
or less went off and made its own kind of

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:54.350
fun house mirror version of journalism. But,
but it forgot to replicate what made journalism,

00:02:54.350 --> 00:03:02.090
uh, work, which is some element of self-correction,
some sense that journalists are devoted to

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:09.220
principles beyond party, beyond faction, that
they're sort of answerable to higher principles

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:15.400
of, of evidence. And, you know, uh, getting
a, a, a second quote, all these sort of day

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:21.100
to day rules of journalism are meant to sort
of guard against tribalism, guard against

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.870
sort of like just telling a story.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:28.010
That's whatever your audience wants to hear.
Uh, when the conservative movement sort of

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:35.060
made its own media, it dropped that part out.
And now we have what is effectively a second

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:44.240
competing media that is devoted just to telling
that audience what it wants to hear, not being

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:52.050
answerable to any principle above partisanship
or above a faction. So, so we have effectively

00:03:52.050 --> 00:04:01.510
become as a, as a people incapable of knowing
things together, which turns out to be really

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:06.620
important to democracy. Really important to
doing anything together. The first step is

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:12.690
knowing things together and we become unable.
We now have a sort of a conservative media

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:22.290
apparatus that if it sets out to can shield
this 30 to 40% of the country from any information

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:28.730
it was, it doesn't matter how clear the information,
it doesn't matter, you know, sort of. Uh,

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:33.350
and I, and I wrote the article about impeachment,
which seems to me sort of a kind of the perfect

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:34.350
example of this.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.830
I mean, the Trump administration cranks out
an example of this every, every day you could

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:44.450
point to, but impeachment, it seems to me,
uh, a poignant example in that the story about

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:51.650
what Trump did, selling, you know, withholding
aid to Ukraine in exchange for an investigation

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:57.960
of, of Biden is just really clear now. It's
just really obvious. We've had multiple, uh,

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:04.440
multiple direct testimony accounts. Trump
has admitted it multiple times before he figured

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.640
out he wasn't supposed to have done it. Multiple
administration officials have admitted to

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.790
it. It's got documentary evidence, there's
a, there's a log of the call on and on and

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:16.900
on. There's just this overwhelming pile of
information. It's just very obvious what he

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:25.350
did and why he did it. And yet, as obvious
as it is, there's 30 to 40% of the country

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:30.430
that doesn't know it, that believes all sorts
of weird talking to any other things about

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:37.040
a giant, deep, deep state conspiracy. And,
uh, you know, a, a server and Ukraine and

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.260
I don't even know all the, all the conspiracy
theories that are going on right now, but,

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:48.520
but the point is we've become incapable of
learning and knowing things together. So it's

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:54.310
not the truth is gone. The truth is out there
as they say. It's not the, it's not the nature

00:05:54.310 --> 00:06:00.850
of truth as a problem. It is a social process
whereby we learn and accept and act on that

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:01.850
truth. That's what's

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:09.560
broken. One of the things that seems to have
helped some of these almost mean type narratives

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:17.110
take hold. Like for example, fake news is
that there's some kernel of sense in the idea,

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:22.320
for example, that Hey, we shouldn't just accept
what media tells us. Okay, great. That there's

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:28.360
a kernel of truth there. Sometimes opinion
and reporting are blurred to your disadvantage.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:33.690
Absolutely. Right. I mean, that's how, that's,
that's a, uh, an important part of what Fox

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:40.220
news does. We should sometimes, uh, media
gets it wrong and so on, but then it becomes,

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:46.710
if it sounds like it's critical of Trump,
it must be untrue without requiring more examination.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:51.520
And I think that's why it's so pernicious
because it starts with a kernel of responsibility

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:54.330
or truth and then goes completely off the
rails.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:59.730
Yeah, no, I think that's totally right. And
I think if you go back and look at postwar

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:07.510
journalism in the U S you know, the conservative
movements critique was always that this journalist

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:14.100
class, especially as as the decades went on,
this sort of composed of people who come from

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:18.040
the liberal class, they're socially liberal.
They're basically, you know, they believe

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:25.590
in tolerance, they're, you know, moderates,
whatever. And that sometimes shades news coverage

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:32.070
in a way where conservatives felt like people
of their persuasion were not getting a voice.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:37.410
There was always a kernel of truth to that.
But the question is, what do you do with that?

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:42.530
If, if, if, if the, if the problem is media
bias that sometimes ends up telling us that

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:48.120
it ends up going against us. What's the solution?
You can go two directions. You could go, let's

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:53.990
redouble out the, the, the rules and principles
that we use to guide journalism.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:59.889
Let's redouble our commitment to fairness.
Let's redouble our commitment to hearing all

00:07:59.889 --> 00:08:05.919
voices and balancing all voices, right? Or
you could go the other way. Here's a bias

00:08:05.919 --> 00:08:10.810
news source that's telling us that's going
against us. Let's create a new source that

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:16.270
tells us what we want to hear, right? So the
problem that they identified, if you look

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:20.540
at what a, if you look at how they reacted,
right? They didn't react by going and creating

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:28.270
media with better guards against bias, right?
Against or better guards against unfairness.

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:32.250
They went and created media that dispense
with all guards against fairness and just

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:36.361
told conservatives what they wanted to hear,
right? So it's sad that looking back on it,

00:08:36.361 --> 00:08:43.869
it sounds to me like it wasn't the bias that
was the problem. It was that the bias went

00:08:43.869 --> 00:08:48.930
in the wrong direction for them. Right? Like
if you look at CNN and your complaint is that

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:55.569
it's not objective enough and then you go
create Fox, right? Maybe it wasn't the, the

00:08:55.569 --> 00:09:00.619
objectivity, that was really the root of your
objection, right? More of the root of their

00:09:00.619 --> 00:09:06.310
objection is just that we're not hearing what
we want. Like the, like the results, what

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:11.731
we're hearing go go against us. So we need,
you know, we're not going to change our, our

00:09:11.731 --> 00:09:16.230
persuasion or what we want or our goals. We're
going to go find a media that will tell us

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:17.290
what we want.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:23.170
I want to get your take on the degree to which
you think education is, is a, is a factor

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:27.699
here. And I'll give you an example that might
be a good lens into it. So the Trump tax reform

00:09:27.699 --> 00:09:32.930
plan passed a couple of years ago and one
of the things that it did is not only did

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:37.790
it change tax rates, but it also changed the
default withholding tables. Meaning that when

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:42.809
you fill out your w four for your employer,
you put, I have one dependent, the default

00:09:42.809 --> 00:09:46.970
amount of tax that was withheld during the
year changed and you very quickly had a lot

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:52.199
of people saying the tax cuts worked. I've
got more money in my paycheck. And then at

00:09:52.199 --> 00:09:57.670
the end of the year you might've had someone
who had $100 more in their paycheck per month,

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:05.860
but their tax return shows $1,500 short. So
in reality they lost $300 net. I have, I have

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:12.029
argued that if we start teaching critical
thinking, basic financial skills, epistemology

00:10:12.029 --> 00:10:18.920
early, like I say at age 12 in seventh grade,
that a lot of those very base talking points

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:24.839
that are effective in politics would be far
less effective. Am I overstating the importance

00:10:24.839 --> 00:10:26.420
of the education piece or,

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:31.050
yeah. This is a really interesting question.
It goes way back and I've been, I've gone

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:39.610
back and forth on this. I have, I have come
down not on the other side. Obviously teaching

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:44.380
young people to think critically is a great
thing. I mean obviously, I mean I don't know

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:49.559
that anybody would be against it. I do think
it's a fantastic idea and I do think especially

00:10:49.559 --> 00:10:55.040
in today's, you know, media and information
environment, it's just a juggle. So absolutely

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:01.680
people need to be better equipped to, to navigate
it, but I ultimately don't think that we're

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:09.679
ever going to get to a place where we have
created a kind of a like a super soldiers

00:11:09.679 --> 00:11:15.910
who become, you know, immune to this stuff
or, or, or can't be fooled by those stuff.

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:22.679
Ultimately, I think relying on individuals
to navigate this is, is fruitless because

00:11:22.679 --> 00:11:27.839
all individuals ultimately, no matter how
good of skeptical thinkers they are, everyone

00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:32.769
is subject to these kinds of biases and these
kinds of cognitive flaws.

00:11:32.769 --> 00:11:37.559
Everybody wants to be told what they want
to hear. Like that's not a, that's not unique

00:11:37.559 --> 00:11:41.300
to any particular kind of person. Everyone
sort of, especially if you're just in sort

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:47.249
of like the, the rush of your day to day life,
you're just not going to like hone in and

00:11:47.249 --> 00:11:52.329
analyze on every single thing that comes out
of the, the TV, you know, to sort of assess

00:11:52.329 --> 00:11:56.550
it yourself. You just don't have this kind
of cognitive energy or time. So, so to me,

00:11:56.550 --> 00:12:04.509
ultimately the solutions got to be in our
institutions. We've got to build self-correction

00:12:04.509 --> 00:12:12.139
and self checking into the institutional rules.
So for instance, I, I think of science, you

00:12:12.139 --> 00:12:17.029
know, [inaudible], I don't know if you've
ever met scientists. They're not super humans.

00:12:17.029 --> 00:12:20.649
They're not, you know, they're, they're just
normal people. They have in their day to day

00:12:20.649 --> 00:12:23.639
life, they have all the same cognitive biases
we have.

00:12:23.639 --> 00:12:31.899
They're not super reasoners to work through
some of the facts of science. They have the

00:12:31.899 --> 00:12:37.079
rules. It's like Guild. It's like a Guild
almost. There's Guild rules. You have to,

00:12:37.079 --> 00:12:42.350
your paper has to be peer reviewed like everyone
and the whole point of the exercises for everyone

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:47.329
to constantly be questioning everybody else.
If you did that in normal life, you'd be a

00:12:47.329 --> 00:12:52.239
jerk, right? But in science there are these
rules. That's how we do things. And so what

00:12:52.239 --> 00:12:56.699
makes it work is collectively, right? It's
not that any individual scientists and superhero

00:12:56.699 --> 00:13:03.100
is that collectively by correcting one another
and checking one another, you can help eliminate

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:07.560
some of those biases and blind spots and sort
of grow up your way towards the truth. And

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:13.360
it's the same for journalism or sort of name
your institution. It's, it's important to

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:19.279
have good institutions and rules and self-correction
mechanisms built into your institutions.

00:13:19.279 --> 00:13:26.160
Cause if that breaks down, ultimately everybody's
just going to retreat to their tribal corners,

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:30.389
right? You're not going to, they're not going
to reason their way through it. They're just

00:13:30.389 --> 00:13:34.269
going to a tribe that they feel safe with
and say, I'm going to believe what these people

00:13:34.269 --> 00:13:38.069
believe. Right? I mean, people feel anxious
with uncertainty. They feel anxious when there's

00:13:38.069 --> 00:13:43.009
no trusted source of information and they
go looking for comfort. And that's what kind

00:13:43.009 --> 00:13:48.910
of this sort of tribalism is that, that we're
seeing so much today. Once you lose these

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:52.949
trust in these institutions, it kind of becomes
everybody for themselves.

00:13:52.949 --> 00:13:58.220
When you look at the antidotes so to speak,
you're, you're sort of alluding to it here

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:03.069
by saying that there has to be some system
that doesn't depend on any one person having

00:14:03.069 --> 00:14:09.459
the sort of, uh, determination to handle it
themselves or to think through it themselves.

00:14:09.459 --> 00:14:12.829
Can you give us in the limited time we have
left, just a couple of examples of what that

00:14:12.829 --> 00:14:14.850
might look like.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:19.980
Well just take, just take journalism and the
rules of journalism. I mean as you learn them,

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:25.209
like you can't just trust one quote, you need
a second quote to backup your first quote

00:14:25.209 --> 00:14:30.819
because the, the first quote, um, might be
a flawed in some way or it might've just,

00:14:30.819 --> 00:14:34.421
you found a quote that told you what you wanted
to hear and it might be the exception if you

00:14:34.421 --> 00:14:37.989
go talk to other people, you might find other
things. Just little rules like that little

00:14:37.989 --> 00:14:45.709
rules. Like if you find an error in your,
in your story, you publicly publish a correction

00:14:45.709 --> 00:14:50.459
even though it's embarrassing to you and maybe
the institution, it's part of the rules that

00:14:50.459 --> 00:14:55.889
we are transparent when we get things wrong.
Just little bits and pieces like that little

00:14:55.889 --> 00:15:02.819
rule, Guild rules. People see those in operation
and it gives them comfort that there's like

00:15:02.819 --> 00:15:07.029
the structures in place that I can trust what
comes out of this institution.

00:15:07.029 --> 00:15:12.519
And so I really think we need to be doing
two things right now. One is improving the

00:15:12.519 --> 00:15:16.699
institutions, right? Like there was a reason.
There are good reasons people have lost trust

00:15:16.699 --> 00:15:21.749
in our institutions. I think, um, you know
the sense that kind of they're rigged for

00:15:21.749 --> 00:15:26.009
the benefit of the powerful, et cetera, et
cetera. I think all that stuff is, is valid.

00:15:26.009 --> 00:15:33.370
So I think we need to refocus on rule of rule
of law and not have people write in democracy

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:41.619
in institutions to make those rules. And we
need to find some way of, of reaching that

00:15:41.619 --> 00:15:49.129
30 to 40% of people who have sort of hived
off from mainstream society and, and bring

00:15:49.129 --> 00:15:54.290
them somehow back into the fold. Cause I just
don't see how democracy survives if we all

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:59.370
know different things, if we're all effectively
living in different worlds. I just don't think

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:04.040
democracy can, can stumble on very long in
that condition.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:07.649
That is a, that is, that is the great fear
and the great concern we've been speaking

00:16:07.649 --> 00:16:12.899
with David Roberts who's a journalist for
box focusing on climate change in politics.

00:16:12.899 --> 00:16:16.499
Check out his work will be linking to some
of it. David, so great having you on. I really

00:16:16.499 --> 00:16:18.459
appreciate your time. Thanks a lot, David.

